WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.182 --> 00:00:01.030
(upbeat dramatic music)

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.423
- Nature. The most powerful
creative force on earth.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.560
I'm Chef Melissa King.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.440
Cooking has taken me to incredible places,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.470
from TV competitions and celebrity galas,

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.840
to countries around the world.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.420
I'm heading out to places
I've never been before,

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.370
to seek out new experiences

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:25.090
and to create new dishes
inspired by nature.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.760
I'm teaming up with some of
the world's greatest explorers

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.016
to go further than I've ever gone before,

00:00:30.016 --> 00:00:34.120
to push my craft,
creativity and my cooking.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.277
This is, "Tasting Wild."

00:00:39.003 --> 00:00:40.330
(wind whipping)

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.530
Hawaii.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.270
The forces of nature that create

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.940
this lush and diverse ecosystem

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.650
are mind blowing.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:51.160
You can feel the energy and
the raw power of the islands

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.050
the moment you land.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.633
I wanna understand where
that feeling comes from,

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.300
take it all in,

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.840
and tell that story by
creating an original dish.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.720
That's why I'm meeting
National Geographic Explorer,

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:05.563
Andres Ruzo.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.110
Andres is a volcanologist,

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.210
who studied here on the Big Island,

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.128
and he'll be my guide to
this remarkable location.

00:01:13.128 --> 00:01:16.480
(gravel crackling)

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.350
Hello, hello!

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.637
- Melissa, I am so glad
to finally meet you!

00:01:20.637 --> 00:01:21.580
- How are you?

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:22.413
- Great.

00:01:22.413 --> 00:01:24.100
Welcome to the biggest active volcano,

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.240
from base to top,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.110
on planet Earth.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.310
My name is Andres Ruzo.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.320
And I'm a National Geographic Explorer,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.760
working in geothermal science,

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.620
conservation and education.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.410
I'm excited to see
Hawaii through her eyes,

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.360
see how she takes this in,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:38.980
and puts her experience on a plate.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.050
- I'm so excited to
see what grows out here

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.970
and just what we can
take inspiration from.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:44.410
I've never been on a volcano before.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.730
- One of the best ways, I think,

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.470
to experience geology is through flavors.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.180
There's some things that
I think you might like.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.440
- All right. I'm ready for it.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.579
- Let's do it.

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:52.450
(gentle music)

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.490
- How does one become a volcanologist?

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.170
- My family grows coffee,

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.891
and has for the past four generations

00:01:57.891 --> 00:02:01.540
on the side of the Casita
Volcano in Nicaragua,

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.510
and I would spend my summers
on the farm and like,

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:07.030
there's hot springs and
super heated waters.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.650
- Wow. You grew up on a volcano.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:09.697
- Pretty much, yeah.

00:02:09.697 --> 00:02:10.958
- That's like the coolest thing ever.

00:02:10.958 --> 00:02:11.800
(car vrooming)

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.600
I've been to different islands in Hawaii,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.460
but this is my first
time on the Big Island.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.530
- So what's really crazy about
the Big Island in particular,

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.540
is that you have all
these amazing ecozones.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.950
If you go on Hilo side,

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.570
it's much more jungle feel,

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:23.650
it's much wetter,

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.700
it rains a lot more, right?

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.500
So, right now we're on the Kona side.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.870
Even though this looks lush and beautiful,

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.510
it is much drier than we
would be on the eastern side.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.850
- I love discovering the
regionality of cuisine

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.640
and just how different it
can be wherever you are.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.080
The nuances of flavor-

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:38.913
- Totally.

00:02:38.913 --> 00:02:41.260
- Really come from the land and the dirt

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.270
where that product was grown.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:45.400
So yeah, I'm interested
to see what we find,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.140
'cause that's really gonna
be the starting point

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.765
to the dish that I'm creating for you.

00:02:48.765 --> 00:02:49.780
- [Andres] This is where the hike begins.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:50.830
- [Melissa] Let's go!

00:02:51.774 --> 00:02:55.180
(gentle rhythmic music)

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:56.470
- [Andres] You could
definitely feel the rain and-

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:57.697
- I love it.

00:02:57.697 --> 00:02:59.500
It's magical.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.720
- So, before they used
to rely on water wells

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.300
and now they're putting in
rain encatchment systems

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.130
to really take advantage of
every drop of this stuff.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:08.193
- Sustainable and-

00:03:08.193 --> 00:03:09.100
- Absolutely.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.530
- Yeah, it's so important
for me as a chef.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.870
A lot of my inspiration comes from

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.430
people that I meet along the way

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.620
and the flavors that I encounter.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.960
There's just so much happening here.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.280
All the different colors,

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.640
and plants that are growing
all next to each other.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.198
I'm like, having sensory overload.

00:03:26.198 --> 00:03:27.940
(Melissa laughs)

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:28.773
Oh, wow.

00:03:28.773 --> 00:03:29.606
- [Andres] Right?

00:03:29.606 --> 00:03:30.730
- Oh my god.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:31.900
Smell that.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.400
Where's the ginger?

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.200
- Bingo. Ginger.
- Oh, there we go.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.480
It's so incredibly rich here.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.630
- You have rocks being
eroded, being degraded,

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.100
ultimately creating soils.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.120
And as a result,

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.310
I mean, you dig under this mulch

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.010
and you see this richness in the soil-

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:48.843
- Yeah.

00:03:48.843 --> 00:03:50.050
- That's laying the foundation.

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:52.390
- I feel like a kid in
a candy store right now.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:53.950
I see passion fruit right there.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.313
They're ripe when they fall.

00:03:55.313 --> 00:03:56.146
- [Andres] Oh yeah!

00:03:56.146 --> 00:03:56.979
- [Melissa] Right there.

00:03:56.979 --> 00:03:57.812
- [Andres] Good eye.

00:03:57.812 --> 00:03:59.303
- Ah, it's such an intoxicating fruit-

00:03:59.303 --> 00:04:00.136
[Andres] Mm hm.

00:04:00.136 --> 00:04:00.969
- Like nasturtiums,

00:04:00.969 --> 00:04:03.706
but it has a really
nice pepperiness to it.

00:04:03.706 --> 00:04:04.539
- You can eat this one too?

00:04:04.539 --> 00:04:05.950
- You can eat that too, yeah.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:07.192
It's almost like a watercress.

00:04:07.192 --> 00:04:08.025
(plants crunching)

00:04:08.025 --> 00:04:08.858
- Yeah.

00:04:08.858 --> 00:04:10.203
- There's a little pepperiness to it.

00:04:10.203 --> 00:04:11.152
- [Andres] Yeah. This one's-

00:04:11.152 --> 00:04:12.700
- And the blossoms you can
create like, a vinegar.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.830
And so, there's a lot of fun
things you can do with this.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:15.700
Are these limes?

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.010
I feel like every culture uses citrus.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.740
It adds some acidity to
their dish and brightness.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:21.573
All right,

00:04:21.573 --> 00:04:24.100
I have so many ingredients
here to work with.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.266
I think we're ready to go.

00:04:25.266 --> 00:04:26.943
- And I wanna take you
to the core of the island

00:04:26.943 --> 00:04:28.870
where all of this magic really starts, so,

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:29.703
let's get going.

00:04:29.703 --> 00:04:31.150
This is a really weird question for you.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.230
Have you ever cooked bugs?

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:33.520
- [Melissa] That's the one
thing that freaks me out.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:34.723
- [Andres] Yeah.

00:04:34.723 --> 00:04:35.556
(Melissa laughs)

00:04:35.556 --> 00:04:36.389
- We're staying off of bugs today.

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:37.433
(Andres and Melissa laugh)

00:04:37.433 --> 00:04:38.526
(car vrooming)

00:04:38.526 --> 00:04:40.480
(upbeat rhythmic music)

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.580
The massive scale of this volcano

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.043
is truly something that I never imagined.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:49.561
I wish everyone could get out
and explore spaces like this.

00:04:49.561 --> 00:04:50.394
(air whooshing)

00:04:50.394 --> 00:04:52.660
The changes of the
weather here are extreme.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.970
We went from sunny and tropical terrain,

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:57.100
to cold, windy sideways rain,

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.200
and now we're here above the clouds.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.260
- [Andres] The Big Island is
full of diverse microclimates

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.670
and we've gotten to experience them

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.620
in a rapid succession on this way up.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:07.330
- [Melissa] Yeah, it's
incredible to experience it

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:08.350
all in one drive.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:10.230
I've never driven anywhere like this.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.860
It looks like we're on the moon.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.990
- Mauna Kea here, from base to top,

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:15.910
it's taller than Everest.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:17.170
Mauna Loa, this is-

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:18.370
- [Melissa] Incredible.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.650
- Both of these are active volcanoes.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.700
We're looking at
beautiful, creative forces,

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:26.590
coming in from deep in the earth,

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.450
forming some of the newest
land on planet Earth.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:30.520
- Well, you can like,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.567
feel the energy, the power of this place.

00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:33.430
(Melissa laughs)

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.050
It's beautiful.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.570
When was the last time this erupted?

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:40.210
- The last major eruption was 1984.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.430
These are living mountains.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:45.610
It's not something
that's just eroding away,

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.280
it's growing, it's active.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.340
This is only a small piece of it.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:56.340
The waters around Hawaii
between 14 to 18,000 feet deep.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.290
What we are on, from here,

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.170
goes all the way down
to those extreme depths,

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:03.760
and it's so heavy,

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.730
it's actually weighing
down the sea floor itself.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:08.590
It is one of the most creative

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:10.300
and inspiring places on earth.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.002
- There's something here
(laughs) that's magnetic.

00:06:13.002 --> 00:06:15.752
(wind whooshing)

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:18.520
- Get on those mountain bikes.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.260
There's a place I really wanna
show you just over there.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:21.310
It's called a lava tube.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:24.790
One of my absolute
favorite geologic features

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:26.320
that you're gonna see on the Big Island,

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.300
volcanic Disney World sort of thing.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:29.590
- [Melissa] Like, we have to see it.

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:30.751
- All right.

00:06:30.751 --> 00:06:32.500
(wind whipping)

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:33.550
Since ancient times,

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.680
Hawaiians have come all the way up here

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.780
to pay their respects to the mountain.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:38.830
- [Melissa] For me as a chef,

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:40.180
a lot of my inspiration

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:43.210
comes from pushing myself as far as I can,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.000
to create dishes that I
never thought I could.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.490
I've never mountain biked
at such a high elevation,

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.680
let alone on a volcano.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:51.760
At this altitude,

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.060
biking across a lava field
is not as easy as it looks.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.133
- So the entrance is right
down here, so we can stop.

00:06:57.133 --> 00:06:57.966
- Stop here.

00:06:57.966 --> 00:06:59.290
- Yeah, this is good.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:02.380
So, I think the best path is over here.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:03.400
So check this out.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.990
As the lava's flowing down here,

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:06.100
you see these ropes.
- Oh, wow.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.670
- It's so hot, it starts
melting its way into the earth.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:11.127
You get a trench like this.

00:07:11.127 --> 00:07:12.730
- [Melissa] Yeah, like sugar pulling.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:13.810
- Exactly, sugar pulling,

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:15.130
like, viscous stuff.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:15.963
So check this out.

00:07:15.963 --> 00:07:17.440
It goes down,

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:21.190
deep enough to where the top part up here

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:23.260
starts to come in on itself,

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:24.892
totally closes over,

00:07:24.892 --> 00:07:26.631
and then it's sealed.

00:07:26.631 --> 00:07:30.010
- This is incredible.
- Now look down there.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:33.730
There's an entire cave system down here

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.511
and this is one of the entrances to it.

00:07:35.511 --> 00:07:36.567
- Oh my god. (laughs)

00:07:36.567 --> 00:07:37.400
- I could go in-

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:38.239
- Oh my god. (laughs)
- And just-

00:07:38.239 --> 00:07:39.893
- Be careful.

00:07:39.893 --> 00:07:43.180
- So I mean, it goes down pretty deep.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:46.180
It's really just a spectacular formation.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.220
- [Melissa] There's like,
plants down there, yeah.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:49.681
- There's these drippings.

00:07:49.681 --> 00:07:51.310
You see them like,

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:54.400
almost like icicles, but of rock.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.130
And this is what ultimately
built the island.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:58.270
- [Melissa] This is incredible.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.276
Never seen anything like this before.

00:08:00.276 --> 00:08:01.810
(gentle music)

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:04.090
Time doesn't exist up here.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:06.401
It's so peaceful and quiet.

00:08:06.401 --> 00:08:09.651
(gentle twinkly music)

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:22.390
- [Andres] Now, wait till
you see the night sky.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:24.780
- [Melissa] So, what
are we gonna see here?

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:26.530
(doors clicking)

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:27.820
- [Andres] We'll start
to see the Milky Way

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:29.748
rise over the next hour.

00:08:29.748 --> 00:08:32.140
(gentle ukulele music)

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:34.420
- [Melissa] This moonroof is so cool.

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:36.831
I just feel so tiny today.

00:08:36.831 --> 00:08:39.940
(Melissa and Andres laughing)

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:43.480
(cymbals glistening)

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.000
Andres is taking me to a black sand beach.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.380
We're gonna see where the lava

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:49.210
goes all the way down to the ocean.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:50.080
I'm really inspired

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:53.177
and I wanna pour some of
that creativity into my dish.

00:08:53.177 --> 00:08:55.870
(waves crashing)

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:58.450
I've never been to a
black sand beach before,

00:08:58.450 --> 00:08:59.860
but what makes it that way?

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:02.770
- When you see different
color sands on beaches,

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.720
it all has to do with the minerals.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:06.520
Lava can turn into rock,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.980
erode away, and turn into sand grains.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:10.060
- [Melissa] Beautiful.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:13.180
- [Andres] I want you to
imagine a massive flow of lava,

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.376
coming down where we were yesterday.

00:09:15.376 --> 00:09:17.170
(waves lapping)

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:20.020
- Wow. It's so much more fine here.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:21.160
- And it's the same rock,

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:22.600
so coming up from deep in the earth,

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.070
coming up with the lavas,

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:25.060
it's the same minerals.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:26.260
- Oh, man.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:27.820
- You'll even see these lime green,

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:29.320
they're called olivine crystals.

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:30.520
- [Melissa] Those are beautiful.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:31.353
- [Andres] And just over here,

00:09:31.353 --> 00:09:32.770
you can see where the actual lava

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:35.260
flowed into the water itself.

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.090
- [Melissa] Trekking
across this hardened lava,

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.940
that originated deep beneath
the surface of the planet,

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:41.860
makes you feel connected to the earth.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:44.110
And seeing the top of
Mauna Loa in the distance

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:45.280
where we were last night,

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:47.260
you can feel the massive
scale of the island,

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:49.870
and the full creative force of nature.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:50.703
- And what's cool,

00:09:50.703 --> 00:09:52.720
is it's all part of the lava's journey,

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.880
from deep in the earth to the surface,

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:57.130
and the force is so hard

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:00.010
that it's getting erosively reworked

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.620
to start its next chapter in the cycle.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:04.150
And you can really see how the waves

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:07.802
are working all of the stones,
smoothing everything out.

00:10:07.802 --> 00:10:09.100
(waves crashing)

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:10.570
- Thank you so much for sharing

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:14.290
this incredible moment with
me and all of your knowledge.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.750
I'm really excited to
just take it all with me

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.400
and share my gifts with you,

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.080
so let's go and cook a little bit.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.060
- [Andres] Oh, I can't wait.

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:23.800
- I'm gonna go to the car
and grab a few things,

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:26.005
but I'll meet you down by the beach.

00:10:26.005 --> 00:10:26.838
(cymbals glistening)

00:10:26.838 --> 00:10:28.180
I really hope that this dish captures

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:30.610
just the power and the
spirit of this island

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.860
from the volcano and the farm,

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:35.354
and all the beautiful things
that we've seen along the way.

00:10:35.354 --> 00:10:37.330
- [Andres] Look at that
magic happening here.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:38.900
This looks great!

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.530
- I decided to make a crudo today,

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:44.140
that's inspired by the
flavors that we've gathered

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.390
over the past couple days.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.040
A crudo is a raw dish,

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:50.560
typically seafood with a seasoned broth.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.360
It feels like the perfect way to reflect

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.876
the raw, creative force
that I feel on this island.

00:10:54.876 --> 00:10:55.810
(gentle piano music)

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:56.643
- I can't wait to taste this.

00:10:56.643 --> 00:10:57.476
(Melissa laughs)

00:10:57.476 --> 00:10:58.309
It sounds amazing.

00:10:58.309 --> 00:11:01.570
- As a chef, you can't
just put food on a plate.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:05.500
It goes deeper down to the story of why.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.720
Why am I using that ingredient?

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:10.180
We should start with
something fresh and bright,

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.460
and there's so many different
colors of the passion fruit

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:14.350
from the red to the yellow,

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:15.850
and smell that.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:16.807
- Oh, that smells so good.

00:11:16.807 --> 00:11:18.670
- And like, the little black seeds

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:20.230
kinda remind me of the lava rocks.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.160
And passion fruit

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:24.040
is one of my favorite
fruits on this island.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:28.090
Scoop that right into our crudo liquid.

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:32.140
Calamansi, it's almost like a kumquat.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:34.157
And look at this fresh
ginger root that we got.

00:11:34.157 --> 00:11:34.990
- Oh, yeah, that's-

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:37.483
- I mean, we have to put
some of that in there.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:42.040
While we were exploring,

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:45.250
I took some time to cure
some local fish here.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.817
It's called a nenue fish.

00:11:46.817 --> 00:11:47.868
[Andres] Oh, wow.

00:11:47.868 --> 00:11:48.701
- It's a white fish,

00:11:48.701 --> 00:11:49.780
and I cured it in kombu.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:51.940
This is just some seaweed
that we picked up,

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.170
right in the ocean,

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:54.672
cured it just for a few hours.

00:11:54.672 --> 00:11:58.005
(gentle powerful music)

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:00.610
- I think it's so beautiful
to see all that like,

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:03.520
filtering through you to create this.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:04.750
- [Melissa] It all comes together

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:07.000
when you travel to a new place,

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.280
and experience the colors and the flavors

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.720
of what's around you.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:12.460
- So you've never made this before?

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:14.320
- I have never made this before.

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:16.210
It's really something that I think

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:18.100
is what's great about being a chef,

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:19.847
is that every dish can change, so.

00:12:19.847 --> 00:12:20.680
- [Andres] So what is that?

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.240
- This is pickled shallots.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:23.770
You have to add a little bit of texture

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:24.940
when it comes to crudo,

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.280
and so I pickled them, I sliced 'em,

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:28.960
there's some daikon-
- Ooh.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:30.673
- That was just sliced thinly.

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:33.259
So I thought adding a
few of these sea beans

00:12:33.259 --> 00:12:35.320
would kind of tie it all together

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:37.810
from the volcano to the ocean,

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:41.440
and those nasturtiums that
we picked up at the farm

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.900
also add a pepperiness to things.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:47.260
Some lava salt, add a
little pop to the dish.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:48.670
Final touch,

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:49.960
we're gonna add some heat here.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.154
It's a Szechuan chili oil.

00:12:52.154 --> 00:12:52.987
- [Andres] Ooh.

00:12:52.987 --> 00:12:56.950
- To me, it was inspired
by the lava and volcano.

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:58.330
- [Andres] That's such a beautiful color.

00:12:58.330 --> 00:12:59.230
It really is like that.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:00.093
It's like a liquid sunset.

00:13:00.093 --> 00:13:01.444
I mean, I love it.

00:13:01.444 --> 00:13:02.277
(Melissa laughs)

00:13:02.277 --> 00:13:03.110
- [Melissa] Liquid sunset.

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:04.120
- You've got me so excited to try this.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.562
- And so, that's the dish.

00:13:05.562 --> 00:13:06.470
(Melissa laughs)

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:07.683
- [Andres] Boom. I love it.

00:13:07.683 --> 00:13:10.938
(gentle twinkly music)

00:13:10.938 --> 00:13:12.443
- [Melissa] All right, let's eat.

00:13:12.443 --> 00:13:14.890
- [Andres] This looks great!

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:15.723
- Please have a seat.

00:13:15.723 --> 00:13:16.977
- Look at you.

00:13:16.977 --> 00:13:18.769
- [Melissa] Here's a little hibiscus tea.

00:13:18.769 --> 00:13:19.602
(waves crashing)

00:13:19.602 --> 00:13:20.500
- Oh, thank you so much for this.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:21.985
- [Melissa] Yeah, of course.

00:13:21.985 --> 00:13:23.063
(gentle music)

00:13:23.063 --> 00:13:24.160
- This is so good.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:26.770
- Really, my take of
everything I saw on the island.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:28.540
- The colors you brought out,

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:30.190
and the pairing of that chili oil

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:31.924
with the passion fruit as well?

00:13:31.924 --> 00:13:33.533
- That's the Chinese side of me.

00:13:33.533 --> 00:13:34.804
- It's a party.

00:13:34.804 --> 00:13:36.730
- This place truly inspired

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:38.860
a lot of new flavors and techniques.

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:39.700
Thank you for that.

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:41.666
- I gotta take you out
on more field adventures.

00:13:41.666 --> 00:13:42.624
(Melissa laughs)

00:13:42.624 --> 00:13:44.796
I'm just sad 'cause I finished this.

00:13:44.796 --> 00:13:46.240
- I'll get you more, don't worry.

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:47.187
Cheers.

00:13:47.187 --> 00:13:48.020
(Melissa laughs)

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:48.853
- Cheers.

00:13:48.853 --> 00:13:52.210
(playful ukulele music)

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:53.920
- [Melissa] This journey
on Hawaii's Big Island

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:55.360
has been such a gift.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.330
It's impossible not to be
struck by the magical beauty,

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:01.300
massive scale and creative forces here.

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:02.980
I'm grateful for the adventure

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:04.571
and inspired by the experience.

00:14:04.571 --> 00:14:07.821
(gentle ukulele music)

00:14:08.735 --> 00:14:10.768
(birds tweeting)

00:14:10.768 --> 00:14:13.172
(car purring)

